Hi Murray,

Thank you so much, we really appreciate your expertise in this.

By looking at everything, our team is going with the realized N of “our sampling area” mask (both for model fit and region.N function) as expected N like you suggested is a good idea but the estimate is much lower than the minimum individuals. Since all tigers 85 were captured within “our sampling area” the lower number 74.5 is not acceptable by the managers.

We also believe that all the tigers with their activity centers lie within “our sampling area” except 3 individuals which probably had activity center outside/inside the area. So we are going with this.

Thank you again,

Sunny day,

Best wishes,

Samundra

From: secr...@googlegroups.com [mailto:secr...@googlegroups.com] On Behalf Of Murray Efford
Sent: Thursday, July 12, 2018 5:38 PM
To: secr <secr...@googlegroups.com>
Subject: Re: secr

What we call 'realised N' is calculated by adding the number of actually detected animals n to a model-based estimate of the number of animals from the region of interest that were not detected.

Realised N is not defined and not computable if you cannot be sure that all the n animals belong in the region for which you wish to know N. If realised N is not defined then CL etc. for realised N are not defined. I cannot change that.

The solution is to use the 'expected N'. The wider CL honestly reflects the fact that you do not really know e.g., whether each tiger you are observe is Nepalese or Indian.

Murray

